Functional Requirements
System Management (Dashboard & Booking)
● Authentication: The system shall allow users to register, log in, and log out as either
an Admin or a Player.
● Player Capabilities:
○ The system shall allow a logged-in Player to view available escape rooms.
○ The system shall allow a Player to create an appointment/reservation and
specify team members.
○ The system shall allow a Player to immediately launch an available game
session.
● Admin Capabilities:
○ The system shall provide a dashboard for Admins displaying business data,
past games, and currently active games with live progress and scores.
○ The system shall allow an Admin to view room details, including specific
puzzles, resolution steps, and required resources.
○ The system shall allow Admins to manage, reorganize, and cancel schedule
reservations.
● Game Administration: The system shall calculate and display the final score and
completion time to both the Player (in-game) and the Admin (in the dashboard) upon
game completion.
Escape Room Client (Game Experience)
● Interactive Puzzles: The game shall present on-screen objects that display puzzle
messages when interacted with (e.g., via mouse clicks).
● Assistance: The game shall provide a mechanism for players to request hints or
help during a session.
● Exit Mechanism: The game shall provide an interface option for players to
voluntarily leave the room before the time expires.
Non-Functional Requirements
To meet the criteria in Chapter 3, phrases like "run flawless" and "look smooth" must be
translated into quantifiable specifications.
● Capacity & Performance: The management backend shall concurrently host and
track live data for up to 3 active escape rooms without degradation.
● Response Time: The system shall initiate and load a new game session in under 5
seconds from the moment a Player clicks "start".
● Availability: The backend management system shall maintain 99.9% uptime to
support constant reservation access.
● Client Performance: The escape room game client shall maintain a minimum
rendering speed of 60 frames per second (FPS) to ensure smooth visual
performance.
Architecture & Tech Stack
Separating the management system from the game client is a standard and highly effective
architecture.
For the backend and management dashboard, leveraging the TypeScript ecosystem with
Bun, Hono, React, PostgreSQL, and Drizzle ORM provides a fast, type-safe environment
that perfectly handles the CRUD operations for reservations and dashboard analytics.
For the escape room itself, building a dedicated client in Unity with C# is a very strong
choice for a 2D point-and-click setup. A pixel-art perspective—perhaps with a crisp,
top-down visual style akin to Enter the Gungeon—fits naturally with mouse-driven object
interactions and pop-up puzzle mechanics. Unity handles complex state logic and asset
management much more cleanly than a DOM-based web framework when building spatial
puzzles. You can then connect the Unity WebGL build or standalone client to your Hono
backend via REST APIs to report scores, request hints, and verify session tokens
